and so it's on months of debate about  the future of our relationship with  Europe now over the course of this  campaign you're going to hear all sorts  of claims and counterclaims and even the  odd bit of dodgy information so this  calls for a survival guide how to  navigate your way through the brexit  campaign and so here are the three big  rules first but where the big eye  catching catch all numbers let's start  with one you've probably already heard  from the remaining camp and that is the  idea that if we left the EU 3,000 pounds  per household would be lost three  million jobs would be at risk according  to their research sounds like a lot  doesn't it but when you dig into the  numbers you find that that 3,000 pounds  is the total economic activity  associated with EU trade no one even the  biggest pessimist expects that to  disappear overnight but this isn't the  only fishy claim out there another one  you've probably heard from the leaf  campaign is the idea that EU membership  cost the UK a whopping 20 billion pounds  a year again sounds like a lot doesn't  it but when you dig into the actual  research you find that first of all 20  billion is kind of overstating it should  be closer to 17 point nine billion  second of all when you account for all  the money coming back from the EU to the  UK the net cost of EU membership is  actually about eight point four billion  pounds still not to be sniffed at but  nowhere near as much as they claim so  survival rule number one but where the  big numbers rule number two but where  the small numbers to my particular  bugbear here are all those job losses  claims you know the kind of thing HSBC  saying a thousand jobs are going to go  over to France you want do a bank  reconsidering whether their headquarters  are going to be here in the UK an Airbus  thinking about kind of the same thing  now the precedent suggests that those  threats don't normally get followed  through but on the other side of things  look out for all of those small numbers  about the costs of EU membership you've  got financial regulations environmental  rules crd for solvency too it's very  easy to get blinded by all of this but  don't miss the wood for the trees the  big picture is that even if the UK  became like  way it would still have to adopt almost  ninety-five percent of those regulations  which brings me to rule number three  what's the alternative to coin a phrase  what's the road map neither side in the  brexit debate seems very keen on  explaining precisely what kind of a deal  we'd have if we left will we be like  Norway would we be like Canada that has  an enormous bearing on the costs and  indeed the benefit of brexit but instead  both sides so far seem far more keen on  focusing less on the facts and more on  the fear but don't worry the best  survival tip of all is to ignore all the  emotional stuff and concentrate on the  details after all this is a crucial fork  in Britain's political road does it stay  in the fold or sort off in a totally  different direction at Conway Sky News
 

